<Project>
  <!--
    ============================================================================================
    SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for the supported VintageStory versions: the "VERSION MANIFEST" region
    below is the ONLY place to edit when the version set changes.

    To ADD a version: append its TFM to $(AllGameTfms), add a per-version PropertyGroup (game
    version + folder slug + install env var) to exlib/build/ExpandedLib.props, and add one
    GAME_GE_<ver> line to the capability-constants block in exlib/build/ExpandedLib.targets.
    To PROMOTE a new primary (e.g. when 1.22 becomes legacy and 1.23 arrives): point
    $(CurrentGameTfm)/$(CurrentGameVer) at the new row (leaving the old one in $(AllGameTfms)).

    Nothing in the csprojs or the #if-guarded code changes for a version move - it all derives
    from here:
      * $(CurrentGameTfm)/$(AllGameTfms) - csprojs read these for TargetFramework(s)
      * $(GamePath)/$(GameVer)/$(GameVersion) - per-TFM install, version, modinfo stamp
      * GAME_GE_<major>_<minor> compile constants - defined for every version <= the one being
        built, so code guards on a CAPABILITY THRESHOLD (#if GAME_GE_1_22) not a raw TFM
        (#if NET10_0). A threshold stays correct as newer versions appear: 1.23 is also >= 1.22,
        so a "#if GAME_GE_1_22" current-API path keeps covering it and a "#if !GAME_GE_1_22"
        legacy shim keeps covering only < 1.22 - including 1.22 itself once it is no longer
        primary. (Mods are written against the CURRENT API; a shim bridges an OLDER version up to
        it, so its guard is "version < where-this-API-arrived".)
      * GameInstallEnv assembly metadata - lets runtime code (the test harness resolver) pick the
        matching install without its own #if ladder.

    Only an ACTUAL new API divergence still needs hand-written code: add a GAME_GE_<new> guard +
    the shim. The plumbing never needs per-version edits again.

    NOTE on evaluation: properties are evaluated before items in MSBuild, so the manifest is
    properties (not an item list) to stay robust. $(TargetFramework) is empty during evaluation of
    the default single-target / outer multi-target build (it's only a global property in the
    legacy inner builds), so $(_GameTfm) falls back to the primary TFM.
    ============================================================================================
  -->

  <!-- ===================== VERSION MANIFEST (edit only this region) ===================== -->
  <!-- The repo's own choice of primary TFM and legacy set - set unconditionally, so the guarded
       defaults of the same name in exlib/build/ExpandedLib.props (imported below) find them
       already set and back off. The per-TFM rows (GameVer/GameSlug/GameInstallEnv) and everything
       derived from $(TargetFramework)/$(AssetDomain) live in that file and its .targets
       counterpart instead - see the header there. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <CurrentGameTfm>net10.0</CurrentGameTfm>
    <CurrentGameVer>1.22.0</CurrentGameVer>
    <AllGameTfms>net7.0;net8.0;net10.0</AllGameTfms>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <!-- =================== end VERSION MANIFEST =================== -->

  <!-- The rest of the shared plumbing - GamePath resolution, provisioning, the capability
       constants, the asset globs, LegacyUsings.cs - ships in the ExpandedLib package so a package
       consumer gets it too; source mode imports the same two files so both run identical logic. -->
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)exlib\build\ExpandedLib.props" />

  <!--
    ============================================================================================
    TEST-ONLY SETTINGS, conditioned on the project name ending in ".Tests" (folded in here rather
    than imported from a separate test props, since every project - mod or test - now shares this
    one file).

    Run the test assemblies in parallel across all cores. Applied automatically by
    `dotnet test` / the IDE runner. The scripts/exmod helper runs the per-version suites
    concurrently on top of this.

    Within an assembly, xunit.runner.json turns collection parallelism OFF. Each mod reads its
    config live from a process-global register (IiexValues, SmexValues, HpexValues), and around
    twenty tests retune one mid-run through `Values.Edit`, so a class exercising production code
    can observe another class's temporary values. Such a failure appears only under parallel load
    and passes on every standalone re-run. Assembly-level parallelism is where the wall-clock comes
    from anyway: a whole suite is a few hundred milliseconds.
    ============================================================================================
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="$(MSBuildProjectName.EndsWith('.Tests'))">
    <RunSettingsFilePath>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\infra\test\parallel.runsettings</RunSettingsFilePath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="$(MSBuildProjectName.EndsWith('.Tests'))">
    <Content
      Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\infra\test\xunit.runner.json"
      Link="xunit.runner.json"
      CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest"
    />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    On the legacy target frameworks, bring exlib's LegacyApi/LegacyAnimUtil extension members into
    scope across every test assembly AND the harness (ExpandedLib.Testing), so code written against
    the 1.22 API surface compiles unchanged on 1.20/1.21 - the same thing each mod project does with
    its own LegacyUsings.cs. Without this a test or harness file touching a 1.22-only member (e.g.
    MultiblockStructure.TransformedOffsets, used by the harness's StructureRig) fails the legacy
    build even though the production code it exercises has a working shim.
  -->
  <ItemGroup
    Condition="($(MSBuildProjectName.EndsWith('.Tests')) or $(MSBuildProjectName.EndsWith('.Testing')))
      and '$(IsCurrentGame)' != 'true'"
  >
    <Using Include="ExpandedLib.Legacy" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
